schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Digital Garden
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A knowledge base deliberately grown over time rather than published once
    and frozen — notes are planted rough, tended, and linked to each other,
    with their maturity shown openly.
  source_traditions:
    - Maggie Appleton, "digital-gardeners" (curated resource collection on GitHub)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Framed as "a knowledge commons that admits it's unfinished" — the right
    posture for a knowledge base meant to outlive team turnover, since waiting
    for pages to be "perfect" is the enemy of a living knowledge base. The
    article's practical instruction: mark a page "rough draft" and publish it
    rather than withholding it.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - knowledge commons (the governance layer a digital garden may or may not have)
    - wiki (a digital garden is typically personal/small-team rather than mass-collaborative)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      "rough and published" vs. "polished and withheld" — a flagged half-answer
      is treated as strictly better than a missing one.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "digital garden" as just an aesthetic/tooling choice (Obsidian +
    Quartz) misses the article's point that it is a posture toward maturity
    and honesty about a note's completeness, independent of which tool renders it.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-knowledge-commons.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
